<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro de Cevallos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1715">1715</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro de Cevallos was a Spanish soldier and colonial administrator. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> from 1757 he captured the Portuguese outpost of Colonia del Sacramento in 1762. In 1776 he led a great expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a> island and Colonia again, and became the first viceroy of the new Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-de-cevallos.jpg" alt="Pedro de Cevallos" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_de_Cevallos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pedro Antonio de Cevallos Cortés y Calderón, also spelled Ceballos, was born in Cádiz on 29 June 1715, the son of Juan Antonio Zeballos y Hollos, a knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-alcantara">Order of Alcántara</a>, and Juana María Cortés y Calderón. Orphaned young, he grew up with his maternal grandparents in Don Benito and was educated at the Seminary of Nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. He joined the Regiment of Order of Catalonia as a sublieutenant in 1730 and fought in the War of the Polish Succession, returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as a captain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1741 he raised a regiment at his own expense, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> made him colonel, and the same year he became a knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a>. He led the regiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, became a brigadier in April 1747, and while there was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and was promoted to field marshal. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1749, he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a> of Sagra and Senet. Sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> with an expedition of 1,000 men, he set up a base at São Borja and in 1759 led 5,000 men against the native peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-chaco">Gran Chaco</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> returned Colonia del Sacramento, which he had taken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1763. He handed the governorship to Francisco de Paula Bucareli on 15 August 1766. Missions to the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Parma in 1771 brought him promotion to captain general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a> in August 1772. His expedition of 1776 numbered 116 ships carrying 19,000 soldiers, and it destroyed Colonia del Sacramento.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> was created by royal decree on 1 August 1776 and organised by Cevallos with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> as its capital. Miguel O'Gorman, who came with him, modernised medicine in the region, and the number of officials in Buenos Aires, most of the senior ones <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsulares">peninsulares</a>, rose from 14 in 1767 to 35 in 1778. Succeeded as viceroy by Juan José de Vértiz y Salcedo, Cevallos set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 26 December 1778, on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro de Cevallos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pedro de Cevallos</b> captured Colonia for the last time in 1777, and the Treaty of San Ildefonso that year confirmed Spanish possession.</blockquote><a href="/state/colonia-del-sacramento">Colonia del Sacramento</a></li><li><blockquote>To counter Portuguese expansion and smuggling, Charles III created the Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata in 1776, with Buenos Aires as its capital and <b>Pedro de Cevallos</b> as first viceroy.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cádiz. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1755" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Pedro de Cevallos"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4678 4321h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4678 4321h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1755">What was here in 1755? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
